# Pinned commit of the litlfred/folio-assistant platform.
#
# The content pipeline, the Zod schemas and every QA checker live in that repo,
# reached through the unpinned `folio-assistant/` symlink that
# scripts/setup-folio-assistant.sh creates. So every number this repo quotes —
# "run-validate: N issues", "check-wall-side: M/1190", any qa-sweep count — is a
# function of BOTH the corpus commit and the platform commit. Without a pin only
# the first is visible in `git log`, and two agents on the same corpus commit can
# measure different baselines and both be correct.
#
# That is not hypothetical: on 2026-08-08 corpus 455013fcf5 reported
# "10 issue(s)" against platform e1030e8 and "29 issue(s)" against e3c2d6b, in a
# single session, and two agents spent a round disagreeing about which baseline
# was real. Neither was wrong; the baseline was not well-defined. See bean `vbase`.
#
# Bumping the platform is TWO lines, not one, and this file used to say one.
#
# `.github/workflows/build.yml` selects the platform for the PUBLISHED build via
# its `uses:` line. That line read `@main` until 2026-09-26, so this pin governed
# only what agents measured locally while the published artifact tracked platform
# main — two platforms, one repo, no signal when they disagreed. It is now pinned
# to the same sha as the last line of this file (bean `qou-pwp9`).
#
# So when you bump: edit the sha here AND in `build.yml`, in the same commit, and
# keep them byte-identical. Checkable, so the rule is not only these comments:
#
#     bash scripts/folio-pin-coupling-check.sh      # exit 0 = the two agree
#
# It asserts only that the two LOCAL declarations agree — a drifted pair is a
# defect, an OLD pair is a choice, and it deliberately does not judge currency.
# It is NOT wired into any hook: adding one changes commit-path behaviour, which
# is the owner's call (cf. the 2026-08-25 precision hook, offered blocking and
# declined in favour of warn-only).
#
# Bumping the platform is a deliberate, reviewable edit to those two lines.
# When you bump it, state the before/after issue counts in the commit message —
# a change in count is a platform behaviour change and should be looked at, not
# absorbed silently.
#
# Override for local testing without editing this file:
#     FOLIO_ASSISTANT_REF=<branch|tag|sha> scripts/setup-folio-assistant.sh
#
# Pinned 2026-08-08 at 428ca5c. Against that platform, corpus 9d6fa7f2c8 measured
# `run-validate folio/quantum-observable-universe` → 4 issue(s) (all warnings).
#
# BUMPED 2026-09-07 to df28d02. Both platforms measured against the SAME corpus
# (the 2026-09-07 main, not the 9d6fa7f2c8 above, which is long superseded — a
# before/after taken at different corpus commits would not be a comparison):
#
#     428ca5c  →  6 issue lines printed, "9 issue(s)" reported
#     df28d02  →  3 issue lines printed, "3 issue(s)" reported
#
# The three that clear are the "Unknown field" warnings — `computation` on
# `knot-periodic-table`, `authorNotes` on `tm-interaction-completeness` and on
# `millennium-bounds-via-surreals-intro`. Those fields were being SILENTLY
# DROPPED at the old pin, exactly as AGENTS.md §4d records: four block kinds
# (prose/equation/diagram/table) declared their zod schemas as standalone
# `z.object({...})` instead of extending `BlockBaseSchema`, so they restated a
# subset of the base fields and a non-strict zod object discarded the rest.
# Upstream `a117daa` makes all four extend the base and adds a regression test
# (`scripts/tests/block-base-fields.test.ts`) that fails when any block schema
# falls behind it. So this bump RESTORES author-facing content that the corpus
# already carries on disk and the pinned platform was throwing away.
#
# Note the old platform's print and count DISAGREED by 3 (6 shown, 9 claimed);
# the new one's agree. That is a second, smaller improvement in the same bump.
#
# The 3 remaining are pre-existing and unrelated to the platform: a bibliography
# info line, one lemma without a Lean declaration, and one undefined
# cross-reference. Neither platform resolves them and neither is asked to.
#
# Also in this bump: litlfred/folio-assistant#167, which stops
# `split_sections` silently discarding every character before the first located
# heading. 18 of 623 library documents were keeping under 50% of their text.
#
# BUMPED 2026-09-26 to 37a36ce — and this one was NOT a one-line edit.
#
# 4553 commits since df28d02 (~19 days). In that window the platform RESTRUCTURED:
# `schemas/`, `content/pipeline/`, `adapters/`, `src/` and `viewer/` no longer exist
# at its top level — everything moved under `cat-harness/`. Measured: all five paths
# GONE on main, `cat-harness/schemas` and `cat-harness/content/pipeline` PRESENT.
#
# 147 qou files referenced the old paths (schemas 73, folio/pipeline 54, adapters 4,
# src 9, viewer 7). 77 code/config files were re-pointed to
# `folio-assistant/cat-harness/<x>` IN THE SAME COMMIT as this bump, because the
# rewrite is invalid at the old pin and the old paths are invalid at the new one —
# they cannot land separately.
#
# One hazard checked and found ABSENT: moving the checkout leaves untracked residue
# behind (`schemas/references.ts`, `content/pipeline/script-sidecars/`), so an old
# import path could in principle resolve to 19-day-old code and appear to work.
# Measured: 4 residue files, ZERO stale `.ts`, so the old imports fail loudly. If a
# future bump leaves `.ts` behind, delete it before trusting any green result.
#
# Also found: `qa-sweep` WRITES provenance stamps into the platform checkout
# (30 `content/pipeline/script-sidecars/*.script.json` were dirty). qou cannot push
# there, so that provenance is unrecoverable and the tree is permanently dirty —
# which also blocks `git checkout` of a new pin until stashed. Stashed, not discarded.
